    {
      "id": "imwdlj",
      "label": "tutorial_positions",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "v70o4y",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In this tutorial, I'll teach you how Ren'Py positions things on the screen. But before that, let's learn a little bit about how Python handles numbers."
        },
        {
          "id": "2mk29v",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are two main kinds of numbers in Python: integers and floating point numbers. An integer consists entirely of digits, while a floating point number has a decimal point."
        },
        {
          "id": "7lqm4s",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, 100 is an integer, while 0.5 is a floating point number, or float for short. In this system, there are two zeros: 0 is an integer, and 0.0 is a float."
        },
        {
          "id": "ccyfnd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py uses integers to represent absolute coordinates, and floats to represent fractions of an area with known size."
        },
        {
          "id": "0f472j",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When we're positioning something, the area is usually the entire screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "0h5n0c",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Let me get out of the way, and I'll show you where some positions are."
        },
        {
          "id": "54hvgb",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide eileen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "8h4omr",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "moveoutright",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "7fq5bd",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "p0q49n",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "axusko",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "bkq70s",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The origin is the upper-left corner of the screen. That's where the x position (xpos) and the y position (ypos) are both zero."
        },
        {
          "id": "cvicdm",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "012m5s",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When we increase xpos, we move to the right. So here's an xpos of .5, meaning half the width across the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "f7xcka",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "6audu0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Increasing xpos to 1.0 moves us to the right-hand border of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "43xi96",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "rvjmw2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: We can also use an absolute xpos, which is given in an absolute number of pixels from the left side of the screen. For example, since this window is 1280 pixels across, using an xpos of 640 will return the target to the center of the top row."
        },
        {
          "id": "79y6s2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The y-axis position, or ypos works the same way. Right now, we have a ypos of 0.0."
        },
        {
          "id": "xv25fa",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "cx6w9a",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's a ypos of 0.5."
        },
        {
          "id": "pkgi92",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "cv4v6h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A ypos of 1.0 specifies a position at the bottom of the screen. If you look carefully, you can see the position indicator spinning below the text window."
        },
        {
          "id": "bkote9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Like xpos, ypos can also be an integer. In this case, ypos would give the total number of pixels from the top of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "ftctxt",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ixat8t",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "e: In other words, it's 75%% of the way from the left side, and 25%% of the way from the top.",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "y7fwnk",
              "text": "xpos 1.0 ypos .5",
              "scene": null
            },
            {
              "id": "eezlmm",
              "text": "xpos .75 ypos .25",
              "scene": null
            },
            {
              "id": "ojv1ig",
              "text": "xpos .25 ypos .33",
              "scene": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "i7fkx7",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide pos",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "yyjttz",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "jsjoci",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show anchor:"
        },
        {
          "id": "0n36mx",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "sujfs2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The second position we care about is the anchor. The anchor is a spot on the thing being positioned."
        },
        {
          "id": "1ozold",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, here we have an xanchor of 0.0 and a yanchor of 0.0. It's in the upper-left corner of the logo image."
        },
        {
          "id": "qs89ca",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show anchor:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ij45a2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When we increase the xanchor to 1.0, the anchor moves to the right corner of the image."
        },
        {
          "id": "eo6oqa",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show anchor:"
        },
        {
          "id": "5k42yi",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Similarly, when both xanchor and yanchor are 1.0, the anchor is the bottom-right corner."
        },
        {
          "id": "olb2ji",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "4ecyzh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To place an image on the screen, we need both the position and the anchor."
        },
        {
          "id": "fkqfn5",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "jmjwxw",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show anchor:"
        },
        {
          "id": "7df4s1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: We then line them up, so that both the position and anchor are at the same point on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "04a8s5",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show anchor:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ay56tu",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ib7308",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "via0gz",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When we place both in the upper-left corner, the image moves to the upper-left corner of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "xpd3w7",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show anchor:"
        },
        {
          "id": "peleic",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show pos:"
        },
        {
          "id": "w6snvb",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "qa891a",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: With the right combination of position and anchor, any place on the screen can be specified, without even knowing the size of the image."
        },
        {
          "id": "wq64js",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "965w91",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "none",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "p04nvk",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide anchor",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "a3zia2",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide pos",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "52ha1t",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "4m3znt",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It's often useful to set xpos and xanchor to the same value. We call that xalign, and it gives a fractional position on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "aafu68",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "t5iqvf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, when we set xalign to 0.0, things are aligned to the left side of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "4ro48s",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "j3q3xc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When we set it to 1.0, then we're aligned to the right side of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "0fp80z",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "zf60nc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: And when we set it to 0.5, we're back to the center of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "1gogzm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Setting yalign is similar, except along the y-axis."
        },
        {
          "id": "0cwtgx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Remember that xalign is just setting xpos and xanchor to the same value, and yalign is just setting ypos and yanchor to the same value."
        },
        {
          "id": "jyiydk",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "o5i6cr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The xcenter and ycenter properties position the center of the image.  Here, with xcenter set to .75, the center of the image is three-quarters of the way to the right side of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "f6wgs2",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "2sq0i9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The difference between xalign and xcenter is more obvious when xcenter is 1.0, and the image is halfway off the right side of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "u2o0z9",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "2g17it",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "wcmp0h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are the xoffset and yoffset properties, which are applied after everything else, and offset things to the right or bottom, respectively."
        },
        {
          "id": "pqnmzv",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "purzz5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Of course, you can use negative numbers to offset things to the left and top."
        },
        {
          "id": "at15dh",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo solid:"
        },
        {
          "id": "3uzag3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Lastly, I'll mention that there are combined properties like align, pos, anchor, and center. Align takes a pair of numbers, and sets xalign to the first and yalign to the second. The others are similar."
        },
        {
          "id": "e4ggr7",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "wph166",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "u8p94s",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "jrh2d0",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "moveinright",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "5ymxvs",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Once you understand positions, you can use transformations to move things around the Ren'Py screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "7oh5up",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "a3of8u",
      "label": "tutorial_atl",
      "bg": "bg washington",
      "music": "sunflower-slow-drag.ogg",
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "wnr3k9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py uses transforms to animate, manipulate, and place images. We've already seen the very simplest of transforms in use:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ir0bun",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "right"
        },
        {
          "id": "0bvfvh",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "7y3v9o",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Transforms can be very simple affairs that place the image somewhere on the screen, like the right transform."
        },
        {
          "id": "ndh7i0",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "7o1j5o",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: But transforms can also be far more complicated affairs, that introduce animation and effects into the mix. To demonstrate, let's have a Gratuitous Rock Concert!"
        },
        {
          "id": "fgjm8v",
          "type": "music",
          "music": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "xazyna",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "concert"
        },
        {
          "id": "5km6iq",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "fade",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "38at6d",
          "type": "music",
          "music": "renpyallstars.ogg"
        },
        {
          "id": "wx8dvo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: But first, let's have... a Gratuitous Rock Concert!"
        },
        {
          "id": "3sutav",
          "type": "music",
          "music": "sunflower-slow-drag.ogg"
        },
        {
          "id": "8t4pkr",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "ywen2d",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "7plh9t",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "eqfi77",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: That was a lot of work, but it was built out of small parts."
        },
        {
          "id": "z0erms",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Most transforms in Ren'Py are built using the Animation and Transform Language, or ATL for short."
        },
        {
          "id": "169g2k",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are currently three places where ATL can be used in Ren'Py."
        },
        {
          "id": "mcbpm7",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_image",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "ukrf0d",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen animated",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "obqelk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The first place ATL can be used is as part of an image statement. Instead of a displayable, an image may be defined as a block of ATL code."
        },
        {
          "id": "afj55k",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When used in this way, we have to be sure that ATL includes one or more displayables to actually show."
        },
        {
          "id": "avhq0m",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_transform",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "z4j90v",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "right"
        },
        {
          "id": "qadhyb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The second way is through the use of the transform statement. This assigns the ATL block to a python variable, allowing it to be used in at clauses and inside other transforms."
        },
        {
          "id": "i75glp",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "a69d9v",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "3p5ajp",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, an ATL block can be used as part of a show statement, instead of the at clause."
        },
        {
          "id": "aw8wnv",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "8o2ez7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When ATL is used as part of a show statement, values of properties exist even when the transform is changed. So even though your click stopped the motion, the image remains in the same place."
        },
        {
          "id": "99pwjn",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "8bqke4",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "o7jo9z",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "moveoutleft",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "osgem0",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "j9d6u3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The key to ATL is what we call composability. ATL is made up of relatively simple commands, which can be combined together to create complicated transforms."
        },
        {
          "id": "2ouwdg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Before I explain how ATL works, let me explain what animation and transformation are."
        },
        {
          "id": "cqcamt",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show eileen animated:"
        },
        {
          "id": "sdboee",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Animation is when the displayable being shown changes. For example, right now I am changing my expression."
        },
        {
          "id": "07w810",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "t0yn4w",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show magic:"
        },
        {
          "id": "taurww",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "589lah",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Transformation involves moving or distorting an image. This includes placing it on the screen, zooming it in and out, rotating it, and changing its opacity."
        },
        {
          "id": "cl78gq",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide magic",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "fy6v24",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "v95pxl",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_image",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "ild3gi",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen animated",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "9ceplz",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To introduce ATL, let's start by looking at a simple animation. Here's one that consists of five lines of ATL code, contained within an image statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "dys80b",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To change a displayable, simply mention it on a line of ATL. Here, we're switching back and forth between two images."
        },
        {
          "id": "f6d6vw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Since we're defining an image, the first line of ATL must give a displayable. Otherwise, there would be nothing to show."
        },
        {
          "id": "p9hebs",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The second and fourth lines are pause statements, which cause ATL to wait half a second each before continuing. That's how we give the delay between images."
        },
        {
          "id": "l342d1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The final line is a repeat statement. This causes the current block of ATL to be restarted. You can only have one repeat statement per block."
        },
        {
          "id": "1cafk0",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_image1",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "q1nyrf",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen animated twice",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "l8fctc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If we were to write repeat 2 instead, the animation would loop twice, then stop."
        },
        {
          "id": "nl05ro",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_image2",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "8gopsq",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen animated once",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "690df7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Omitting the repeat statement means that the animation stops once we reach the end of the block of ATL code."
        },
        {
          "id": "a2b6u5",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_with",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "2s6qup",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "bg atl transitions",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "7lxwy2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By default, displayables are replaced instantaneously. We can also use a with clause to give a transition between displayables."
        },
        {
          "id": "ffsg9r",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "bg washington",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "44zpyf",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "rr1ziy",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "zoioj8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: With animation done, we'll see how we can use ATL to transform images, starting with positioning an image on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "375mcq",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base behind eileen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "rvrgqr",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at topright",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "yx57pk",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "1iqh0y",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_transform",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "tjbnfu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The simplest thing we can do is to statically position an image. This is done by giving the names of the position properties, followed by the property values."
        },
        {
          "id": "0gicaa",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_transform1",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "et7qdd",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at move_jump",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "4kh7yw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: With a few more statements, we can move things around on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "d1rylo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This example starts the image off at the top-right of the screen, and waits a second. It then moves it to the left side, waits another second, and repeats."
        },
        {
          "id": "6p4j7w",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The pause and repeat statements are the same statements we used in our animations. They work throughout ATL code."
        },
        {
          "id": "50q2qc",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example atl_transform2",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "8ujokb",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at move_slide",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "k12zu5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Having the image jump around on the screen isn't all that useful. That's why ATL has the interpolation statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "xkcaca",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The interpolation statement allows you to smoothly vary the value of a transform property, from an old to a new value."
        },
        {
          "id": "0mmfe2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here, we have an interpolation statement on the second ATL line. It starts off with the name of a time function, in this case linear."
        },
        {
          "id": "h6jpnn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: That's followed by an amount of time, in this case three seconds. It ends with a list of properties, each followed by its new value."
        },
        {
          "id": "bdwteg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The value of each property is interpolated from its value when the statement starts to the value at the end of the statement. This is done once per frame, allowing smooth animation."
        },
        {
          "id": "3aqw84",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "sfmo93",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "right"
        },
        {
          "id": "aaaaiv",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "gp6jcu",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "bibbko",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: ATL supports more complicated move types, like circle and spline motion. But I won't be showing those here."
        },
        {
          "id": "ww5kar",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo with dissolve",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "r8ff1w",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Apart from displayables, pause, interpolation, and repeat, there are a few other statements we can use as part of ATL."
        },
        {
          "id": "wu72ii",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show eileen happy:"
        },
        {
          "id": "5pbnbt",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 1.25
        },
        {
          "id": "cfv218",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 1.25
        },
        {
          "id": "j5kefe",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "4oi9wc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: ATL transforms created using the statement become ATL statements themselves. Since the default positions are also transforms, this means that we can use left, right, and center inside of an ATL block."
        },
        {
          "id": "i1ihkw",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "h0etk4",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base behind eileen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "bxoxf6",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "8hinve",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "k9eehl",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here, we have two new statements. The block statement allows you to include a block of ATL code. Since the repeat statement applies to blocks, this lets you repeat only part of an ATL transform."
        },
        {
          "id": "wt129z",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: We also have the time statement, which runs after the given number of seconds have elapsed from the start of the block. It will run even if another statement is running, stopping the other statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "cdk1hc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: So this example bounces the image back and forth for eleven and a half seconds, and then moves it to the right side of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "9d7aol",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "i8bkg1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The parallel statement lets us run two blocks of ATL code at the same time."
        },
        {
          "id": "l6m22a",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here, the top block move the image in the horizontal direction, and the bottom block moves it in the vertical direction. Since they're moving at different speeds, it looks like the image is bouncing on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "huqml5",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "wacvg5",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "gan55h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, the choice statement makes Ren'Py randomly pick a block of ATL code. This allows you to add some variation as to what Ren'Py shows."
        },
        {
          "id": "zncygm",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo base",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "d851ma",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "bv57ku",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "8sgaxa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This tutorial game has only scratched the surface of what you can do with ATL. For example, we haven't even covered the on and event statements. For more information, you might want to check out {a=https://renpy.org/doc/html/atl.html}the ATL chapter in the reference manual{/a}."
        },
        {
          "id": "2oy1dh",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "7rg2zn",
      "label": "transform_properties",
      "bg": "bg washington",
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "9g0nmz",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py has quite a few transform properties that can be used with ATL, the Transform displayable, and the add Screen Language statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "yla0w7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here, we'll show them off so you can see them in action and get used to what each does."
        },
        {
          "id": "7fao7s",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "right"
        },
        {
          "id": "gwbn1u",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "73ntx6",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "brjj69",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "3r7m1h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: First off, all of the position properties are also transform properties. These include the pos, anchor, align, center, and offset properties."
        },
        {
          "id": "b6g50j",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide eileen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "icmvc0",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo base",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "e7gbre",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show bg band:"
        },
        {
          "id": "q65mg1",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "rq9opo",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show bg band:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ejkong",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The position properties can also be used to pan over a displayable larger than the screen, by giving xpos and ypos negative values."
        },
        {
          "id": "jrr12s",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show bg band:"
        },
        {
          "id": "aeglr6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "The subpixel property controls how things are lined up with the screen. When False, images can be pixel-perfect, but there can be pixel jumping."
        },
        {
          "id": "omlnly",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show bg band:"
        },
        {
          "id": "7c22cf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "When it's set to True, movement is smoother at the cost of blurring images a little."
        },
        {
          "id": "7rq9fy",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide bg",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "x4as1t",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "bg washington",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "abj7zv",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "right"
        },
        {
          "id": "jiofzn",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "18wpp7",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo small:"
        },
        {
          "id": "p2msd3",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "hsgij1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Transforms also support polar coordinates. The around property sets the center of the coordinate system to coordinates given in pixels."
        },
        {
          "id": "7oazea",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo small:"
        },
        {
          "id": "4rwmwo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The angle property gives the angle in degrees. Angles run clockwise, with the zero angle at the top of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "3nk9o5",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo small:"
        },
        {
          "id": "s5pfun",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The radius property gives the distance in pixels from the anchor of the displayable to the center of the coordinate system."
        },
        {
          "id": "i06c0n",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo small",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "xe9oy6",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "x3cgq9",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "p9rso7",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "dtmn4d",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are several ways to resize a displayable. The zoom property lets us scale a displayable by a factor, making it bigger and smaller."
        },
        {
          "id": "dya1t7",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "o37w1p",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "lkgdtg",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "6kh8x2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The xzoom and yzoom properties allow the displayable to be scaled in the X and Y directions independently."
        },
        {
          "id": "fn70o1",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "czmsj4",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "38mh8n",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "bjai6p",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By making xzoom or yzoom a negative number, we can flip the image horizontally or vertically."
        },
        {
          "id": "vltpfq",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "wann64",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "rk3zn8",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "6dwsbw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Instead of zooming by a scale factor, the size transform property can be used to scale a displayable to a size in pixels."
        },
        {
          "id": "xx7je8",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "tu1zlq",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "tearwn",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "qtxiyn",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "h34iwj",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "k2rcqk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The alpha property is used to change the opacity of a displayable. This can make it appear and disappear."
        },
        {
          "id": "qeyoxi",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "voy8am",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "8xjdkx",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "ia0nil",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The rotate property rotates a displayable."
        },
        {
          "id": "kk5v1y",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "osnwrd",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "q3aarj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By default, when a displayable is rotated, Ren'Py will include extra space on all four sides, so the size doesn't change as it rotates. Here, you can see the extra space on the left and top, and it's also there on the right and bottom."
        },
        {
          "id": "1nbfwz",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ujcqtr",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "s39c70",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By setting rotate_pad to False, we can get rid of the space, at the cost of the size of the displayable changing as it rotates."
        },
        {
          "id": "pfzhoe",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "3wcuzz",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "zwgocj",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "jzsgeh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The tile transform properties, xtile and ytile, repeat the displayable multiple times."
        },
        {
          "id": "fm9231",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at reset",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "ywk8dw",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show logo base:"
        },
        {
          "id": "kfisjf",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "2ni9b6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The crop property crops a rectangle out of a displayable, showing only part of it."
        },
        {
          "id": "3tjn6p",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo base",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "v8pl4b",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "1bf9kp",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show bg washington:"
        },
        {
          "id": "qxo2v4",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "cnq2tf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When used together, crop and size can be used to focus in on specific parts of an image."
        },
        {
          "id": "264ngv",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide bg",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "zkcz22",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show bg panorama:"
        },
        {
          "id": "dl0e49",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "99260q",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The xpan and ypan properties can be used to pan over a displayable, given an angle in degrees, with 0 being the center."
        },
        {
          "id": "wueaiv",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "hr5lq6",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "o1riht",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "1ycom0",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "b2trm0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Those are all the transform properties we have to work with. By putting them together in the right order, you can create complex things."
        },
        {
          "id": "yj706i",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "y6vdch",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p2ftrg",
      "label": "director",
      "bg": "bg washington",
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "wmg9kq",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are a few tools you can access by pressing the right commands on the keyboard."
        },
        {
          "id": "da8i8c",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Typing Shift+R turns on autoreload mode. When it's enabled, your game will automatically reload when you edit a script file."
        },
        {
          "id": "4x1aue",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Shift+O brings you to the console, which lets you enter Ren'Py and Python commands to try them out."
        },
        {
          "id": "e20dkp",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Shift+D pops up a developer menu with access to these and other functions."
        },
        {
          "id": "qfgx91",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The most powerful tool is the interactive director that lets you add images, music, and voice lines to your game from inside Ren'Py."
        },
        {
          "id": "wg6zp1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The idea is that you can use an editor to write the script and logic of your visual novel, and then interactively add images in the right places."
        },
        {
          "id": "j94znn",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen concerned",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "w5i6eq",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It looks like Ren'Py is installed read-only on your system, so you won't be able to try out the interactive director now."
        },
        {
          "id": "y456hc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You'll need to make your own project, and try it out there. But I can tell you how to use it."
        },
        {
          "id": "j5otfu",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "fzrxgf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can try the interactive director out right now, by using it to change this tutorial game."
        },
        {
          "id": "lz9acd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Be sure to click my dialogue at the bottom of the screen to advance the tutorial."
        },
        {
          "id": "o3s0k0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If something goes wrong, don't worry. Quitting and restarting this tutorial will remove your changes and bring everything back to normal."
        },
        {
          "id": "8wh28e",
          "type": "music",
          "music": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "8x7eh1",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "mdlixt",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ _director_enable = not director_readonly"
        },
        {
          "id": "vbcdyx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To get started, let's go back to a blank slate, with no images on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "iviofd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can show the director at any time by pressing the 'D' key on your keyboard. Ren'Py will reload, and you'll come back here. Try it now."
        },
        {
          "id": "ci6zq3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Let's add a background. Click the '+' to pick where to add it, then the 'scene' statement and 'washington' for the image. Finally, click 'Add' to add it."
        },
        {
          "id": "1kgy6u",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Next, add a sprite. Click '+', then 'show', 'eileen', 'happy', and 'Add'. Once you've added it, dissolve it in by clicking the second '+', then 'with', 'dissolve', and 'Add'."
        },
        {
          "id": "gufqi1",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex97xa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can edit or remove statements with the pencil icon. You can move me to the right by editing the show statement, then clicking '(transform)', 'right', and 'Change'."
        },
        {
          "id": "6kry96",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, you can use the play, queue, stop, and voice statements to manage audio. Try adding 'play', 'music', 'sunflower-slow-drag.ogg'."
        },
        {
          "id": "g388to",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ director.state.show_director = False"
        },
        {
          "id": "cqk69z",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ _director_enable = False"
        },
        {
          "id": "5gavsn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "l: Finally, I get some more screen time!"
        },
        {
          "id": "mn7q40",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "uir5o3",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "qdnj1m",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "ae31qa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The changes you make with the director are permanent. They're saved to the script, and you can rollback or repeat this section to see them."
        },
        {
          "id": "7shexm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: However, we reset this tutorial when the game restarts, so you can try again from a clean slate. That won't happen with your own visual novel."
        },
        {
          "id": "94gzs6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: I hope these tools make developing your visual novel that much easier."
        },
        {
          "id": "d38ogk",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "k7m1mw",
      "label": "distribute",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "i6bv5z",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: One thing Ren'Py makes easy is building distributions of your visual novel so you can give it to players."
        },
        {
          "id": "1c809p",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Before you build distributions, you should use the Lint command to check your game for problems."
        },
        {
          "id": "zy3jx1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While not every potential problem lint reports is a real issue, they generally are, and you should try to understand what might be wrong."
        },
        {
          "id": "0i8h8w",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "launcher distribute at launcher_place",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "vk0hrk",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "moveinleft",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "kt5lro",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: After lint has finished, you can choose Build Distributions to build the Windows, Linux, and Mac distributions of your game."
        },
        {
          "id": "7oatrn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This can be as simple as clicking the Build button, when you're not on a Mac."
        },
        {
          "id": "4m9bin",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If you are on a Macintosh, you can have Ren'Py sign the Mac application, which makes it easier for players to run. To enable this, you need to set build.mac_identity in options.rpy."
        },
        {
          "id": "e9b3z1",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide launcher distribute",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "0bkhk8",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "moveoutleft",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "ahpcrt",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py supports the mobile platforms, Android and iOS. We also support ChromeOS, through its ability to run Android apps."
        },
        {
          "id": "fl356q",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: These mobile platforms can be a bit more complicated. While Android apps can be built everywhere, iOS requires a Mac."
        },
        {
          "id": "4qvcd6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Mobile platforms might also require you to change your visual novel a little, due to the smaller limited devices. For example, buttons need to be made large enough for a person to touch."
        },
        {
          "id": "ussnex",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Rather than cover mobile here, I'll point you to the {a=https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/android.html}Android{/a} and {a=https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/ios.html}iOS{/a} documentation, where you can read more."
        },
        {
          "id": "pdaaz0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Thanks to the distribution tools Ren'Py ships with, there are thousands of visual novels available."
        },
        {
          "id": "9o1rgm",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen vhappy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "9uxskd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: I hope that soon, yours will be one of them!"
        },
        {
          "id": "v7guxh",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "o2qar4",
      "label": "tutorial_nvlmode",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "vwwdjz",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: NVL-style games are games that cover the full screen with text, rather than placing it in a window at the bottom of the screen. Like this."
        },
        {
          "id": "sn53ii",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example nvl1 bottom",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "7yz7jo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: To use NVL-mode, you need to define Characters with a kind=nvl."
        },
        {
          "id": "md68zo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: Then just use that character in a say statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "hcgsmt",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: You use 'nvl clear' to clear the screen when that becomes necessary."
        },
        {
          "id": "x4oudj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: The 'nvl show' and 'nvl hide' statements use transitions to show and hide the NVL window."
        },
        {
          "id": "n2exld",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ menu = nvl_menu"
        },
        {
          "id": "655gjr",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example nvl3a nvl3 large bottom",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "frkofe",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "nvle: Well, hopefully the code below makes it a little more clear.",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "5fl9bn",
              "text": "Yes.",
              "scene": null
            },
            {
              "id": "bfi6q5",
              "text": "No.",
              "scene": null
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "subpmc",
      "label": "after_nvl_menu",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "1z2q7h",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "zye3wb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: Games can mix NVL-mode and the normal ADV-mode by having some characters that have kind=nvl, and some that do not."
        },
        {
          "id": "ekrirn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can specify transitions that occur when going from NVL-mode to ADV-mode."
        },
        {
          "id": "4t3wdr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: As well as when going from ADV-mode to NVL-mode."
        },
        {
          "id": "3n5cok",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: Text tags like {{w}{w} work in NVL-mode."
        },
        {
          "id": "6qhpvk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "extend:  As does the \\\"extend\\\" special character."
        },
        {
          "id": "3m6i32",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "nvle: And that's it for NVL-mode."
        },
        {
          "id": "lzdo2i",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ menu = renpy.display_menu"
        },
        {
          "id": "xsqr34",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ _last_say_who = None"
        },
        {
          "id": "r3z09t",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "lzsex6",
      "label": "tutorial_playing",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "1k2bl7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: As someone who has played more than a few visual novels, there are many features that I expect all games to have."
        },
        {
          "id": "1blauy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Features like saving, loading, changing preferences, and so on."
        },
        {
          "id": "d3s9qu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: One of the nice things about Ren'Py is that the engine provides many of these features for you. You can spend your time creating your game, and let us provide these things."
        },
        {
          "id": "d9yz27",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While you're in the game, you can access the game menu by right clicking or hitting the escape key."
        },
        {
          "id": "wmx9zq",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can also access the game menu through some of the quick menu buttons at the bottom of this screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "mh0z3j",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "right"
        },
        {
          "id": "2vrjr5",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "popup save at popup_place",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "6hbr6h",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "moveinleft",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "zibfix",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When you first enter the game menu, you'll see the save screen. Clicking on a numbered slot will save the game."
        },
        {
          "id": "1alpgu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Unlike other engines, Ren'Py doesn't limit the number of save slots that you can use. You can keep hitting next until you reach the page you want."
        },
        {
          "id": "2scxwi",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Clicking on the title of the page allows you to start typing to change the page name."
        },
        {
          "id": "wuc91a",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The load screen looks quite similar to the save screen, and lets you load a game from a save slot."
        },
        {
          "id": "0xa4l5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It also lets you load one of the auto-saves that Ren'Py makes for you."
        },
        {
          "id": "z7xojx",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "popup prefs at popup_place",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "d0jsjr",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "ibaxss",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The game menu also has the preferences screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "75ax3u",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This screen lets you decide how Ren'Py displays, pick what Ren'Py skips, control text speed and auto-click speed, and adjust sound, music, and voice volumes."
        },
        {
          "id": "shjrk5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The game menu also lets you end the game and return to the main menu, or quit Ren'Py entirely."
        },
        {
          "id": "0t3fth",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "popup hrpprefs at popup_place",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "x4vz0o",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "a3r1u2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While the default game menus look a bit generic, with a little work they can be customized or even entirely replaced, allowing you to create menus as unique as your game."
        },
        {
          "id": "n6c0p7",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide popup",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "ptu38l",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "0pz96n",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "moveoutleft",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "7g6v03",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While inside the game, there are a few more things you can do."
        },
        {
          "id": "z7bc7i",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When I'm liking a visual novel, I want to see all the endings. Ren'Py's skip function lets me easily do this, by skipping text that I've already seen."
        },
        {
          "id": "78y85d",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: I can skip a few lines by holding down Control, or I can toggle skip mode by clicking the skip button at the bottom of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "u1p32h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By default, we only skip read text, so this won't do anything the first time through the game."
        },
        {
          "id": "avew4n",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Clicking the auto button toggles auto-forward mode, which makes the game advance without you clicking."
        },
        {
          "id": "b26wsv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The Q.Save and Q.Load buttons provide a single-click way to make a save, and a fast way to load that save again."
        },
        {
          "id": "y2t5sm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Pressing the 's' key saves a screenshot to disk, so I can upload pictures of the game to websites like {a=https://www.renpy.org}renpy.org{/a}."
        },
        {
          "id": "da5kh6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The history button displays a history of read text - but you can also use rollback, which is usually better."
        },
        {
          "id": "dj9s0n",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "e: Would you like to hear about rollback?",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "uz4jsh",
              "text": "Yes.",
              "scene": "rdeaor"
            },
            {
              "id": "54qtz7",
              "text": "No.",
              "scene": "uayhum"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rdeaor",
      "label": "tutorial_rollback",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "ol3rrr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can invoke a rollback by clicking the 'Back' button, scrolling the mouse wheel up, or by pushing the page up key. That'll bring you back to the previous screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "p1x4x0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While at a previous screen, you can roll forward by scrolling the mouse wheel down, or pushing the page down key."
        },
        {
          "id": "n7rj8k",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Rolling forward through a menu will make the same choice you did last time. But you don't have to do that - Ren'Py's rollback system allows you to make a different choice."
        },
        {
          "id": "309taj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can try it by rolling back through the last menu, and saying 'No'."
        },
        {
          "id": "cis645",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Click back a few times, press page up, or scroll up the mouse wheel."
        },
        {
          "id": "1zuls0",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen concerned",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "awsjab",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Well, are you going to try it?"
        },
        {
          "id": "gqrcq3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Your loss."
        },
        {
          "id": "uzla5e",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Moving on."
        },
        {
          "id": "gblac4",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "4edj0r",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "uayhum",
          "transition": "none",
          "_targetLabel": "tutorial_rollback_done"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "uayhum",
      "label": "tutorial_rollback_done",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "3xgruu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By allowing Ren'Py to take care of out-of-game issues like loading and saving, you can focus on making your game, while still giving players the experience they've come to expect when playing visual novels."
        },
        {
          "id": "m27ea4",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "m85f0w",
      "label": "tutorial_create",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "kojn0g",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "launcher step1 at launcher_place",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "i7ntv4",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "bgjg59",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When you're ready to use Ren'Py to create your visual novel, the first step is to create a new project."
        },
        {
          "id": "v5xldm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can create a new project by clicking 'Create New Project' on the front screen of the launcher."
        },
        {
          "id": "jsgmtq",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If this is your first time using Ren'Py, it'll ask you for the place you want to keep your projects. The best place is always somewhere that's frequently backed up."
        },
        {
          "id": "33pqt5",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "launcher step2",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "hi3wfo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: After that, Ren'Py will ask for a name for your project. You'll have to stick to English letters and numbers, as zip files can't handle anything more than that."
        },
        {
          "id": "l1pgxa",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "launcher step3",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "7xlvb5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The next thing Ren'Py will ask for is the resolution the visual novel will run at. This controls how large or small you'll have to make your game's artwork."
        },
        {
          "id": "8bmec1",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "launcher step4",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "1670gg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, Ren'Py will ask you to select a color scheme. You can change this after the game has been created, so just pick a color that's pleasing."
        },
        {
          "id": "p8kbuv",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "launcher step5",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "dvflku",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Once that's done, Ren'Py will work for a bit and return you to the main menu with the new project selected. Now, when you click Launch, Ren'Py will start your new game."
        },
        {
          "id": "rl7ayj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To get back here, you can choose 'Tutorial' to switch to this tutorial game."
        },
        {
          "id": "3joc66",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You'll also need to edit the games script to make changes. To do that, click 'script.rpy' on the front page of the launcher."
        },
        {
          "id": "d2t50y",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If it's your first time doing so, Ren'Py will ask you to select a text editor. Atom might be a safe choice, but read the descriptions to be sure."
        },
        {
          "id": "7ntuzx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: After the text editor is downloaded, the script will open up and you can start to change what characters are saying."
        },
        {
          "id": "9qqh4j",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide launcher",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "2eybaw",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "y6yiht",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "k2jhho",
      "label": "tutorial_dialogue",
      "bg": "bg washington",
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "1ntkzb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Probably the most common thing a creator does with Ren'Py is to write dialogue for the player to read."
        },
        {
          "id": "enmrov",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: But before I can show you how to write dialogue, let me show you how we present script examples."
        },
        {
          "id": "iy6wlv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Eileen\" \"Examples will show up in a window like the one above. You'll need to click outside of the example window in order to advance the tutorial."
        },
        {
          "id": "5247eh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Eileen\" \"When an example is bigger than the screen, you can scroll around in it using the mouse wheel or by simply dragging the mouse."
        },
        {
          "id": "o1ant7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Eileen\" \"Script might seem scary at first, but if you look you'll see it's easy to match it up to what I'm saying."
        },
        {
          "id": "u49ju6",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "wwvjo3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Let's see the simplest possible Ren'Py game."
        },
        {
          "id": "dgnce9",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "black"
        },
        {
          "id": "047184",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "6phna6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Wow, it's really really dark in here."
        },
        {
          "id": "saou20",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Lucy\" \"Better watch out. You don't want to be eaten by a Grue."
        },
        {
          "id": "x4ms52",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "qv8eyd",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "900rci",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "3xlhnl",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example start dialogue1",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "xdlbkh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: I'll show you the script of that example."
        },
        {
          "id": "ov9b00",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This script demonstrates two kinds of Ren'Py statements, labels and say statements."
        },
        {
          "id": "mlr9tc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The first line is a label statement. The label statement is used to give a name to a place in the program."
        },
        {
          "id": "kv52fw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In this case, we're naming a place \\\"start\\\". The start label is special, as it marks the place a game begins running."
        },
        {
          "id": "2cpj5d",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The next line is a simple say statement. It consists of a string beginning with a double-quote, and ending at the next double-quote."
        },
        {
          "id": "dcnssk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Special characters in strings can be escaped with a backslash. To include \\\" in a string, we have to write \\\\\\\"."
        },
        {
          "id": "onjtj7",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "5mjat5",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "black"
        },
        {
          "id": "mwfkg8",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "1p360x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Wow, it's really really dark in here."
        },
        {
          "id": "72hx9f",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "760cwv",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "v3vz3j",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "gqs61m",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example start dialogue1",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "6aiu82",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When Ren'Py sees a single string on a line by itself, it uses the narrator to say that string. So a single string can be used to express a character's thoughts."
        },
        {
          "id": "epnex4",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "6d1dyd",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "black"
        },
        {
          "id": "vbq91i",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "5qqvnu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Lucy\" \"Better watch out. You don't want to be eaten by a Grue."
        },
        {
          "id": "z743w0",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "9fzw9t",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "7zy0ly",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "390aeq",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example start dialogue1",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "nya27a",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When we have two strings separated by a space, the first is used as the character's name, and the second is what the character is saying."
        },
        {
          "id": "duozbf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This two-argument form of the say statement is used for dialogue, where a character is speaking out loud."
        },
        {
          "id": "3daf9h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If you'd like, you can run this game yourself by erasing everything in your project's script.rpy file, and replacing it with the code in the box above."
        },
        {
          "id": "gnvtil",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Be sure to preserve the spacing before lines. That's known as indentation, and it's used to help Ren'Py group lines of script into blocks."
        },
        {
          "id": "p1gyyz",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "f6vk80",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Using a string for a character's name is inconvenient, for two reasons."
        },
        {
          "id": "8bfm6u",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The first is that it's a bit verbose. While typing \\\"Lucy\\\" isn't so bad, imagine if you had to type \\\"Eileen Richardson\\\" thousands of times."
        },
        {
          "id": "eflhdl",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The second is that it doesn't leave any place to put styling, which can change the look of a character."
        },
        {
          "id": "wgcd1f",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To solve these problems, Ren'Py lets you define Characters."
        },
        {
          "id": "xzyyss",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example characters",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "dauphj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's an example Character definition. It begins with the word \\\"define\\\". That tells Ren'Py that we are defining something."
        },
        {
          "id": "zrmcou",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Define is followed by a short name for the character, like \\\"l\\\". We'll be able to use that short name when writing dialogue."
        },
        {
          "id": "y01qd5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This is followed by an equals sign, and the thing that we're defining. In this case, it's a Character."
        },
        {
          "id": "20xoe8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: On the first line, the character's name is given to be \\\"Lucy\\\", and her name will be drawn a reddish color."
        },
        {
          "id": "t3jrvd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: These short names are case-sensitive. Capital L is a different name from lower-case l, so you'll need to be careful about that."
        },
        {
          "id": "jokpaw",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "ot9sb0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Now that we have a character defined, we can use it to say dialogue."
        },
        {
          "id": "vd64hr",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "black"
        },
        {
          "id": "dcxf5c",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "s2sen3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "l: Why are you trying to put words into my mouth? And who are you calling \\\"it\\\"?"
        },
        {
          "id": "1og2pj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "l: What's more, what are you going to do about the Grue problem? Are you just going to leave me here?"
        },
        {
          "id": "kdyer4",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "tg0p3e",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "j6hlfr",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "bvukod",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example characters start dialogue1 dialogue2",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "j78hcg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's the full game, including the two new lines of dialogue, both of which use the Character we defined to say dialogue."
        },
        {
          "id": "gr1gmn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The one-argument form of the say statement is unchanged, but in the two-argument form, instead of the first string we can use a short name."
        },
        {
          "id": "9qtj65",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When this say statement is run, Ren'Py will look up the short name, which is really a Python variable. It will then use the associated Character to show the dialogue."
        },
        {
          "id": "tnlyk4",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The Character object controls who is speaking, the color of their name, and many other properties of the dialogue."
        },
        {
          "id": "5zm7qt",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "ie13jg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Since the bulk of a visual novel is dialogue, we've tried to make it as easy to write as possible."
        },
        {
          "id": "puxpp7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Hopefully, by allowing the use of short names for characters, we've succeeded."
        },
        {
          "id": "loyrz8",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "iyjk8x",
      "label": "tutorial_images",
      "bg": "bg washington",
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "8p3p7j",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A visual novel isn't much without images. So let's add some images to our little game."
        },
        {
          "id": "w0fipn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Before we can show images, we must first choose image names, then place the image files into the images directory."
        },
        {
          "id": "wf1lco",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: An image name is something like 'bg cave' or 'lucy happy', with one or more parts separated by spaces."
        },
        {
          "id": "mjcl3e",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Each part should start with a lower-case letter, and then contain lower-case letters, numbers, and underscores."
        },
        {
          "id": "s23023",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The first part of an image is called the tag. For 'bg cave' the tag is 'bg', while for 'lucy happy' the tag is 'lucy'."
        },
        {
          "id": "tk59n8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can open the images directory by clicking the appropriate button in the Ren'Py launcher."
        },
        {
          "id": "ca5y30",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The files in the images directory should have the same name as the image, followed by an extension like .jpg, .png, or .webp."
        },
        {
          "id": "a1vm0k",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Our example uses 'bg cave.jpg', 'lucy happy.png', and 'lucy mad.png'."
        },
        {
          "id": "vz4907",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "cevzdi",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Let's see what those look like in the game."
        },
        {
          "id": "527zhu",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg cave"
        },
        {
          "id": "2jygrn",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "lucy happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "2a4f7l",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "l: Now that the lights are on, we don't have to worry about Grues anymore."
        },
        {
          "id": "j9owpd",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "lucy mad",
          "side": "right"
        },
        {
          "id": "3coiae",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "l: But what's the deal with me being in a cave? Eileen gets to be out in the sun, and I'm stuck here!"
        },
        {
          "id": "ptz6lb",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "g8zjpq",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "c0wfnf",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "5qi6l4",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example start images1 large",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "xk3hj3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's the script for that scene. Notice how it includes two new statements, the scene and show statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "m2j9yo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The scene statement clears the screen, and then adds a background image."
        },
        {
          "id": "3hkmlv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The show statement adds a new image on top of all the other images on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "26gfd9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If there is already an image with the same tag, the new image is used to replace the old one."
        },
        {
          "id": "w7wk92",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Changes to the list of shown images take place instantly, so in the example, the user won't see the background by itself."
        },
        {
          "id": "pw2p7o",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The second show statement has an at clause, which gives a location on the screen. Common locations are left, right, and center, but you can define many more."
        },
        {
          "id": "kl2rlq",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "logo base at rightish behind eileen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "24tjbo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In this example, we show an image named logo base, and we show it at a creator-defined position, rightish."
        },
        {
          "id": "o7a67r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: We also specify that it should be shown behind another image, in this case eileen. That's me."
        },
        {
          "id": "e5q0al",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide logo",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "tmwjaj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, there's the hide statement, which hides the image with the given tag."
        },
        {
          "id": "x54pj8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Since the show statement replaces an image, and the scene statement clears the scene, it's pretty rare to hide an image."
        },
        {
          "id": "df2vew",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The main use is for when a character or prop leaves before the scene is over."
        },
        {
          "id": "wojpi1",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "glbxd5",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "j90ee3",
      "label": "tutorial_simple_positions",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "atzde0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When the standard positions that come with Ren'Py aren't enough for you, you can create your own. Here, I'll show you the easy way to do it."
        },
        {
          "id": "c234vo",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show eileen happy:"
        },
        {
          "id": "7clvst",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "jyuo4m",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The first way to do it is to show an image followed by a colon. Then indented on the next couple of lines are the xalign and yalign transform properties."
        },
        {
          "id": "gxa56d",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Each of the transform properties is a name followed by a value. For xalign and yalign, the values are numbers."
        },
        {
          "id": "2fmyqe",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The xalign transform property is the important one, as it controls where the image is placed horizontally on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "4hgbox",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show eileen happy:"
        },
        {
          "id": "sdm7qa",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "d4eml8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: An xalign of 0.0 is the left side."
        },
        {
          "id": "ulvu3z",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show eileen happy:"
        },
        {
          "id": "4r1he0",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "psli0p",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: 0.5 is the center."
        },
        {
          "id": "wbiosb",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show eileen happy:"
        },
        {
          "id": "79dana",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "qybcft",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: And 1.0 is the right. The decimal place is important and has to be there. Just 1 by itself won't work the same."
        },
        {
          "id": "rs47yg",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show eileen happy:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ecfnnu",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "8lsw49",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Of course, you can pick any position in between."
        },
        {
          "id": "7bz9zk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The yalign property is the same way, with 0.0 being the top of the screen and 1.0 being the bottom. Since most sprites stick to the bottom, it's almost always 1.0."
        },
        {
          "id": "csgg52",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "b7onlh",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "1q8szn",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "5spse9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While being able to write positions like this is useful, having to repeatedly do so isn't. So Ren'Py lets you define a transform once, and reuse it."
        },
        {
          "id": "hcxw61",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example slightleft",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "r0nvhl",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Usually transforms are defined at the top of a file, right after the characters. But it doesn't matter to Ren'Py where you define them."
        },
        {
          "id": "0wttj8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The transform is given a name, slightleft, and then the xalign and yalign properties."
        },
        {
          "id": "fi436g",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen vhappy at slightleft",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "0g6buu",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "move",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "7g5t0s",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Once a transform has been defined, you can use it in the at clause of the show statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "f4cony",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "gidwoi",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Transforms are sticky. If you replace an image without using a transform, Ren'Py will keep the same transforms it had been using."
        },
        {
          "id": "gp0pm2",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "n88var",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Of course, there's a lot more to transforms than this. If you want to learn more, you can read the sections on Position Properties, Transforms and Animation, and Transform Properties."
        },
        {
          "id": "pxsu9r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: But for many visual novels, xalign and yalign are the only properties that matter."
        },
        {
          "id": "rd1t5v",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p47c63",
      "label": "tutorial_transitions",
      "bg": "bg washington",
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "zcymwx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It can be somewhat jarring for the game to jump from place to place."
        },
        {
          "id": "6f7bxm",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg whitehouse"
        },
        {
          "id": "ucyr54",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "vacbfe",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "5ird5q",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "67eam8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To help take some of edge off a change in scene, Ren'Py supports the use of transitions. Let's try that scene change again, but this time we'll use transitions."
        },
        {
          "id": "md8a5c",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg whitehouse"
        },
        {
          "id": "1v8mex",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "Dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "cfg8x6",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "762873",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "lzo8k9",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "y3k6ny",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "Dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "mip22x",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example trans1 large",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "ma9mux",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: That's much smoother. Here's some example code showing how we include transitions in our game."
        },
        {
          "id": "shj3sc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It uses the with statement. The with statement causes the scene to transition from the last things shown to the things currently being shown."
        },
        {
          "id": "md5g7a",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It takes a transition as an argument. In this case, we're using the Dissolve transition. This transition takes as an argument the amount of time the dissolve should take."
        },
        {
          "id": "zh64le",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In this case, each transition takes half a second."
        },
        {
          "id": "j0a47v",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example slowdissolve",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "8y04xw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: We can define a short name for a transition, using the define statement. Here, we're defining slowdissolve to be a dissolve that takes a whole second."
        },
        {
          "id": "ixrxt3",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "m9kt5m",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg whitehouse"
        },
        {
          "id": "eqitrm",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "slowdissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "axr8eb",
          "type": "bg",
          "bg": "bg washington"
        },
        {
          "id": "ykbyoq",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "lus39w",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "slowdissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "t079pg",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example trans2",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "n2a8jd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Once a transition has been given a short name, we can use it in our game."
        },
        {
          "id": "epxqyb",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "hegige",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py defines some transitions for you, like dissolve, fade, and move. For more complex or customized transitions, you'll have to define your own."
        },
        {
          "id": "j65yy7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If you're interested, check out the Transitions Gallery section of this tutorial."
        },
        {
          "id": "i3ofvh",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "m9us5x",
      "label": "tutorial_music",
      "bg": null,
      "music": "sunflower-slow-drag.ogg",
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "zutrm5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Another important part of a visual novel or simulation game is the soundtrack."
        },
        {
          "id": "c0n7ti",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py breaks sound up into channels. The channel a sound is played on determines if the sound loops, and if it is saved and restored with the game."
        },
        {
          "id": "ew838b",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When a sound is played on the music channel, it is looped, and it is saved when the game is saved."
        },
        {
          "id": "ck3p42",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When the channel named sound is used, the sound is played once and then stopped. It isn't saved."
        },
        {
          "id": "gha3iy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The sounds themselves are stored in audio files. Ren'Py supports the Opus, Ogg Vorbis, and mp3 formats."
        },
        {
          "id": "ghrjw5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Let's check out some of the commands that can affect the music channel."
        },
        {
          "id": "6gwks2",
          "type": "music",
          "music": "sunflower-slow-drag.ogg"
        },
        {
          "id": "c5wy8i",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The play music command replaces the currently playing music, and replaces it with the named filename."
        },
        {
          "id": "mgf0r5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If you specify the currently-playing song, it will restart it."
        },
        {
          "id": "xjx363",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: If the optional fadeout clause is given, it will fade out the currently playing music before starting the new music."
        },
        {
          "id": "sy3n66",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The queue statement also adds music to the named channel, but it waits until the currently-playing song is finished before playing the new music."
        },
        {
          "id": "p2eh8e",
          "type": "music",
          "music": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "hlc4ee",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The third statement is the stop statement. It stops the music playing on a channel. It too takes the fadeout clause."
        },
        {
          "id": "zgrm8z",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "play sound \"tower_clock.ogg\""
        },
        {
          "id": "een8iw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Unlike the music channel, playing a sound on the sound channel causes it to play only once."
        },
        {
          "id": "ebf2sj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You can queue up multiple sounds on the sound channel, but the sounds will only play one at a time."
        },
        {
          "id": "z75dm7",
          "type": "music",
          "music": "sunflower-slow-drag.ogg"
        },
        {
          "id": "f5sbt6",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "68njfh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py has separate mixers for sound, music, and voices, so the player can adjust them as they like."
        },
        {
          "id": "ge5u73",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "clgazj",
      "label": "tutorial_menus",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "u2eysb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Many visual novels require the player to make choices from in-game menus. These choices can add some challenge to the game, or adjust it to the player's preferences."
        },
        {
          "id": "cgk61m",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Do you like to play visual novels with choices in them?"
        },
        {
          "id": "wrg7qj",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "6b5req",
              "text": "Yes, I do.",
              "scene": "1jumoo"
            },
            {
              "id": "bajaut",
              "text": "No, I don't.",
              "scene": "ue25b2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1jumoo",
      "label": "choice1_yes",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "xru27o",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ menu_flag = True"
        },
        {
          "id": "axg631",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While creating a multi-path visual novel can be a bit more work, it can yield a unique experience."
        },
        {
          "id": "gijbx4",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "zb2y8q",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "choice1_done"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ue25b2",
      "label": "choice1_no",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "jel6dc",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ menu_flag = False"
        },
        {
          "id": "pa70nq",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Games without menus are called kinetic novels, and there are dozens of them available to play."
        },
        {
          "id": "04sr71",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "zb2y8q",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "choice1_done"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "zb2y8q",
      "label": "choice1_done",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "9nohkk",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example menu1 large",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "jp8d5p",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here, you can see the code for that menu. If you scroll down, you can see the code we run after the menu."
        },
        {
          "id": "psj4df",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Menus are introduced by the menu statement. The menu statement takes an indented block, in which there can be one line of dialogue and multiple choices."
        },
        {
          "id": "mhvvpt",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Each choice must end with a colon, as each choice has its own block of Ren'Py code, that is run when that choice is selected."
        },
        {
          "id": "4xe63s",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here, each block jumps to a label. While you could put small amounts of Ren'Py code inside a menu label, it's probably good practice to usually jump to a bigger block of code."
        },
        {
          "id": "9ie9xw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Scrolling down past the menu, you can see the labels that the menu jumps to. There are three labels here, named choice1_yes, choice1_no, and choice1_done."
        },
        {
          "id": "8pwhmg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When the first menu choice is picked, we jump to the choice1_yes, which runs two lines of script before jumping to choice1_done."
        },
        {
          "id": "1yqnzt",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Similarly, picking the second choice jumps us to choice1_no, which also runs two lines of script."
        },
        {
          "id": "yftdnk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The lines beginning with the dollar sign are lines of python code, which are used to set a flag based on the user's choice."
        },
        {
          "id": "s27s4y",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The flag is named menu_flag, and it's set to True or False based on the user's choice. The if statement can be used to test a flag, so the game can remember the user's choices."
        },
        {
          "id": "7xs7gh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, I remember that you plan to use menus in your game."
        },
        {
          "id": "8fsp0r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, I remember that you're planning to make a kinetic novel, without menus."
        },
        {
          "id": "71i56x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's an example that shows how we can test a flag, and do different things if it is true or not."
        },
        {
          "id": "46gy0l",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "e: If you look at the example, before the first choice, there's an indented say statement.",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "9tvryg",
              "text": "Yes.",
              "scene": null
            },
            {
              "id": "cqmz9f",
              "text": "No.",
              "scene": null
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "687xyv",
      "label": "menu3_done",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "582t41",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "8ql0s5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Although we won't demonstrate it here, Ren'Py supports making decisions based on a combinations of points, flags, and other factors."
        },
        {
          "id": "h1h2yh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: One of Ren'Py's big advantages is the flexibility using a scripting language like Python provides us. It lets us easily scale from kinetic novels to complex simulation games."
        },
        {
          "id": "h138pw",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "88jom8",
      "label": "tutorial_input",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "so3lmw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Some games might prompt the player for input."
        },
        {
          "id": "2tjm4e",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example input",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "z6r9ag",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: That's done with Python, and especially the renpy.input function. The first line of this example prompts the player for some texts, and sticks it in the name variable."
        },
        {
          "id": "326fse",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Often times, you'll want to clean the name up before you use it. The last line does that, by calling the strip method to remove whitespace, and replacing the name with a default if it's missing."
        },
        {
          "id": "wda32z",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To interpolate a variable, write it in square brackets. Isn't that right, [name]?"
        },
        {
          "id": "00swie",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example guy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "ptnsxo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Variable names can also be shown in character names. To do that, just include the variable in square brackets in the character's name. Got it?"
        },
        {
          "id": "qith2h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "g: I think I do."
        },
        {
          "id": "b0u8b3",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ answer = 42"
        },
        {
          "id": "0994ew",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ flag = True"
        },
        {
          "id": "wlx9x9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Variable interpolation also works with other variables. Here, the answer is [answer] and the flag is [flag]."
        },
        {
          "id": "hd6vls",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        },
        {
          "id": "oetkrs",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: [name!t]"
        },
        {
          "id": "jpqyyu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text:  [hp]/[hp_max]"
        },
        {
          "id": "tskxlt",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "style_prefix: stat"
        },
        {
          "id": "1lras3",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "byh79h",
          "transition": "none",
          "_targetLabel": "tutorial_screens"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "byh79h",
      "label": "tutorial_screens",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "gsgiik",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Screens are the most powerful part of Ren'Py. Screens let you customize the out-of-game interface, and create new in-game interface components."
        },
        {
          "id": "cbupjc",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "m4ez3g",
          "transition": "none",
          "_targetLabel": "screens_menu"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "m4ez3g",
      "label": "screens_menu",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "0u39mv",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ reset_example()"
        },
        {
          "id": "y58ghn",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "e: What would you like to know about screens?",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "zind90",
              "text": "What screens can do.",
              "scene": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "wc27yp",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "4fizr5",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screens_demo"
        },
        {
          "id": "bbfpom",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "How to show screens."
        },
        {
          "id": "eazrzg",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "1vg25t",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screens_showing"
        },
        {
          "id": "mcqyyk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Passing parameters to screens."
        },
        {
          "id": "kqwpnr",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "p2uguy",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screens_parameters"
        },
        {
          "id": "cygwxn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Screen properties."
        },
        {
          "id": "kczjve",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "nsdw8q",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screens_properties"
        },
        {
          "id": "vqhrgy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Special screen statements."
        },
        {
          "id": "3m36ng",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "luh6yg",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screens_control"
        },
        {
          "id": "9pxe33",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Using other screens."
        },
        {
          "id": "96qb7e",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "slprr3",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screen_use"
        },
        {
          "id": "vwsmcd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "That's it."
        },
        {
          "id": "9g1rzk",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        },
        {
          "id": "jndj28",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "m4ez3g",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screens_menu"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "4fizr5",
      "label": "screens_demo",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "3qnhrr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Screens are how we create the user interface in Ren'Py. With the exception of images and transitions, everything you see comes from a screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "vcnm61",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When I'm speaking to you, I'm using the 'say' screen. It's responsible for taking dialogue and presenting it to the player."
        },
        {
          "id": "mqwh6q",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "e: You aren't limited to these screens either. In Ren'Py, you can make your own screens, and use them for your game's interface.",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "j40maa",
              "text": "Yes.",
              "scene": null
            },
            {
              "id": "dz1m0m",
              "text": "I do.",
              "scene": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "50he4g",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ player_hp = 15"
        },
        {
          "id": "x08sx3",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "screen stats",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "ts879k",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "5cko4x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, in an RPG like visual novel, a screen can display the player's statistics."
        },
        {
          "id": "0d9nuu",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ player_hp = 42"
        },
        {
          "id": "tkkcx8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Which reminds me, I should probably heal you."
        },
        {
          "id": "4up9bx",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen stats",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "5265ob",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "screen day_planner",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "j2nsf3",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "hexgzs",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Complex screens can be the basis of whole game mechanics. A stats screen like this can be the basis of dating and life-sims."
        },
        {
          "id": "fnomuk",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen day_planner",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "ulaoyt",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "j5rsuf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While screens might be complex, they're really just the result of a lot of simple parts working together to make something larger than all of them."
        },
        {
          "id": "ppktxb",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1vg25t",
      "label": "screens_showing",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "u0yy15",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's an example of a very simple screen. The screen statement is used to tell Ren'Py this is a screen, and its name is simple_screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "cnpc0x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Inside the screen statement, lines introduces displayables such as frame, vbox, text, and textbutton; or properties like action, xalign, and ypos."
        },
        {
          "id": "jqm4xk",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "screen simple_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "mpjdqr",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "730nea",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: I'll work from the inside out to describe the statements. But first, I'll show the screen so you can see it in action."
        },
        {
          "id": "gfek4b",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The text statement is used to display the text provided."
        },
        {
          "id": "z4mt0p",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The textbutton statement introduces a button that can be clicked. When the button is clicked, the provided action is run."
        },
        {
          "id": "rtpjry",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Both are inside a vbox, which means vertical box, statement - that places the text on top of the button."
        },
        {
          "id": "c5skto",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: And that is inside a frame that provides the background and borders. The frame has an at property that takes a transform giving its position."
        },
        {
          "id": "2pz74k",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen simple_screen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "d03n44",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "9i6n6g",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "wl1m9u",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are a trio of statements that are used to display screens."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3ygan",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "screen simple_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "gcoiir",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The first is the show screen statement, which displays a screen and lets Ren'Py keep going."
        },
        {
          "id": "i21q43",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The screen will stay shown until it is hidden."
        },
        {
          "id": "uaxb9r",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen simple_screen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "3hkk3l",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Hiding a screen is done with the hide screen statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "00h9cm",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example call_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "58oyyx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The call screen statement stops Ren'Py from executing script until the screen either returns a value, or jumps the script somewhere else."
        },
        {
          "id": "txfe6z",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Since we can't display dialogue at the same time, you'll have to click 'Okay' to continue."
        },
        {
          "id": "lopym0",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "09vg6h",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screen"
        },
        {
          "id": "wp7mv7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When a call screen statement ends, the screen is automatically hidden."
        },
        {
          "id": "sfvwq7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Generally, you use show screen to show overlays that are up all the time, and call screen to show screens the player interacts with for a little while."
        },
        {
          "id": "w23kzk",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p2uguy",
      "label": "screens_parameters",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "ym0lbh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: [message!t]"
        },
        {
          "id": "fbx4hw",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "znjgh9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's an example of a screen that takes three parameters. The message parameter is a message to show, while the okay and cancel actions are run when the appropriate button is chosen."
        },
        {
          "id": "cl9tll",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: While the message parameter always has to be supplied, the okay and cancel parameters have default values that are used if no argument is given."
        },
        {
          "id": "71r0j4",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Each parameter is a variable that is defined inside the screen. Inside the screen, these variables take priority over those used in the rest of Ren'Py."
        },
        {
          "id": "gvheat",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example show_parameter_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "slxv79",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When a screen is shown, arguments can be supplied for each of the parameters. Arguments can be given by position or by name."
        },
        {
          "id": "mw10ki",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "1ufsnn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Parameters let us change what a screen displays, simply by re-showing it with different arguments."
        },
        {
          "id": "cddore",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen parameter_screen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "so10tk",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "mwwlmj",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example call_parameter_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "f6w1an",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The call screen statement can also take arguments, much like show screen does."
        },
        {
          "id": "97c90t",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "k04vso",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screen"
        },
        {
          "id": "5v2kac",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nsdw8q",
      "label": "screens_properties",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "gatt7d",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are a few properties that can be applied to a screen itself."
        },
        {
          "id": "t91zhg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When the modal property is true, you can't interact with things beneath the screen. You'll have to click 'Close This Screen' before you can continue."
        },
        {
          "id": "d0k8pk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When a screen has the tag property, it's treated like the tag part of an image name. Here, I'm showing a_tag_screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "qjy4zy",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "screen b_tag_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "0r54r5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When I show b_tag_screen, it replaces a_tag_screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "k4t67x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This is useful in the game and main menus, where you want the load screen to replace the preferences screen. By default, all those screens have tag menu."
        },
        {
          "id": "jp2svl",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen concerned",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "qnk4v4",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For some reason, tag takes a name, and not an expression. It's too late to change it."
        },
        {
          "id": "sa192k",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "eileen happy",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "bewf6n",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "none",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "us99gh",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen b_tag_screen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "cqsq0y",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: Zorder 100"
        },
        {
          "id": "doadrn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: Zorder 0"
        },
        {
          "id": "ctaszb",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "screen zorder_100_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "gon19y",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "screen zorder_0_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "5kfzz9",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "qhq90a",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The zorder property controls the order in which screens overlap each other. The larger the zorder number, the closer the screen is to the player."
        },
        {
          "id": "ntm6zj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By default, a screen has a zorder of 0. When two screens have the same zorder number, the screen that is shown second is closer to the player."
        },
        {
          "id": "bwpppg",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen zorder_100_screen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "jntrmn",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen zorder_0_screen",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "6bq832",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "variant: small"
        },
        {
          "id": "xe062i",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The variant property selects a screen based on the properties of the device it's running on."
        },
        {
          "id": "v8rkmi",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In this example, the first screen will be used for small devices like telephones, and the other screen will be used for tablets and computers."
        },
        {
          "id": "ncazcp",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "style_prefix: red"
        },
        {
          "id": "279w4u",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "background: #440000d9"
        },
        {
          "id": "61h5c9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "color: #ffc0c0"
        },
        {
          "id": "l3j1xe",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, the style_prefix property specifies a prefix that's applied to the styles in the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "4sgp4x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When the 'red' prefix is given, the frame gets the 'red_frame' style, and the text gets the 'red_text' style."
        },
        {
          "id": "psdk1x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This can save a lot of typing when styling screens with many displayables in them."
        },
        {
          "id": "sdunjr",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "y0siqa",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "022paf",
      "label": "warp_screen_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "14slmv",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ renpy.pop_call()"
        },
        {
          "id": "t3ro74",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "aio0oj",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screen_displayables"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "luh6yg",
      "label": "screens_control",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "8ea8jm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The screen language has a few statements that do things other than show displayables. If you haven't seen the section on {a=jump:warp_screen_displayables}Screen Displayables{/a} yet, you might want to check it out, then come back here."
        },
        {
          "id": "2hosm1",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ message = _(\"Hello, World.\")"
        },
        {
          "id": "3ocpx0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: [message!t]"
        },
        {
          "id": "yqisop",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The python statement works just about the same way it does in the script. A single line of Python is introduced with a dollar sign. This line is run each time the screen updates."
        },
        {
          "id": "yc5x99",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: [message1!t]"
        },
        {
          "id": "csc9yx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: [message2!t]"
        },
        {
          "id": "1ob5sa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Similarly, the python statement introduces an indented block of python statements. But there is one big difference in Python in screens and Python in scripts."
        },
        {
          "id": "jhuzao",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The Python you use in screens isn't allowed to have side effects. That means that it can't do things like change the value of a variable."
        },
        {
          "id": "se9h4g",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The reason for this is that Ren'Py will run a screen, and the Python in it, during screen prediction."
        },
        {
          "id": "1xl3vc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: n = [n]"
        },
        {
          "id": "1b8msx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The default statement lets you set the value of a screen variable the first time the screen runs. This value can be changed with the SetScreenVariable and ToggleScreenVariable actions."
        },
        {
          "id": "0h4vgb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The default statement differs from the Python statement in that it is only run once. Python runs each time the screen updates, and hence the variable would never change value."
        },
        {
          "id": "q9xhfy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: n = [n]\" color \"#cfc"
        },
        {
          "id": "wwmjup",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: n = [n]\" color \"#fcc"
        },
        {
          "id": "8pskho",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The if statement works like it does in script, running one block if the condition is true and another if the condition is false."
        },
        {
          "id": "6q87ed",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ landings = [ _(\"Earth\"), _(\"Moon\"), _(\"Mars\") ]"
        },
        {
          "id": "xfnx8e",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "textbutton: [i!t]"
        },
        {
          "id": "ufwr2v",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The for statement takes a list of values, and iterates through them, running the block inside the for loop with the variable bound to each list item."
        },
        {
          "id": "w0bi63",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "on: show\" action Notify(_(\"The screen was just shown."
        },
        {
          "id": "nnwmoc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "key: a\" action Notify(_(\"You pressed the 'a' key."
        },
        {
          "id": "9coruf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The on and key statements probably only make sense at the top level of the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "e5yo8t",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The on statement makes the screen run an action when an event occurs. The 'show' event happens when the screen is first shown, and the 'hide' event happens when it is hidden."
        },
        {
          "id": "7hwgo2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The key event runs an event when a key is pressed."
        },
        {
          "id": "88h7vu",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "2zdsup",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "slprr3",
      "label": "screen_use",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "14v1oa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The screen language use statement lets you include a screen inside another. This can be useful to prevent duplication inside screens."
        },
        {
          "id": "zq4yms",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Take for example this screen, which shows two stat entries. There's already a lot of duplication there, and if we had more stats, there would be more."
        },
        {
          "id": "ioyysj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here, we moved the statements that show the text and bar into a second screen, and the use statement includes that screen in the first one."
        },
        {
          "id": "69aqe8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The name and amount of the stat are passed in as arguments to the screen, just as is done in the call screen statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "k5nxom",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By doing it this way, we control the amount of duplication, and can change the stat in one place."
        },
        {
          "id": "vp0sjm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The transclude statement goes one step further, by letting the use statement take a block of screen language statements."
        },
        {
          "id": "ohzhuo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When the included screen reaches the transclude statement it is replaced with the block from the use statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "mnxm50",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The boilerplate screen is included in the first one, and the text from the first screen is transcluded into the boilerplate screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "p5yxqd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Use and transclude are complex, but very powerful. If you think about it, 'use boilerplate' is only one step removed from writing your own Screen Language statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "411t16",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "s9w8cz",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "aio0oj",
      "label": "screen_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "3ive8u",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are quite a few screen displayables. Here, I'll tell you about some of the most important ones."
        },
        {
          "id": "tysyxc",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "4tl47v",
          "transition": "none",
          "_targetLabel": "screen_displayables_menu"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "4tl47v",
      "label": "screen_displayables_menu",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "y4zdkh",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ reset_example()"
        },
        {
          "id": "ci7isf",
          "type": "choice",
          "prompt": "e: What would you like to know about?",
          "opts": [
            {
              "id": "x2vg1c",
              "text": "Common properties all displayables share.",
              "scene": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "px7iiv",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "fayxjj",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screen_displayable_properties"
        },
        {
          "id": "3lqvi9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Adding images and other displayables."
        },
        {
          "id": "2eafo4",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "fon0j7",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "add_displayable"
        },
        {
          "id": "necr8e",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Text."
        },
        {
          "id": "044qz1",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "rvq3ar",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "text_displayable"
        },
        {
          "id": "gsn276",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Boxes and other layouts."
        },
        {
          "id": "8vrfgn",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "115x6j",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "layout_displayables"
        },
        {
          "id": "p9r7ty",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Windows and frames."
        },
        {
          "id": "gcz3it",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "093yak",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "window_displayables"
        },
        {
          "id": "nc4r1g",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Buttons."
        },
        {
          "id": "b9ymhc",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "yqplo3",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "button_displayables"
        },
        {
          "id": "tqmdpr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Bars."
        },
        {
          "id": "nnjj2y",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "7bpxvn",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "bar_displayables"
        },
        {
          "id": "gy0g21",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Viewports."
        },
        {
          "id": "3iu2bt",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "nkaa56",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "viewport_displayables"
        },
        {
          "id": "k2x7qa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "Imagemaps."
        },
        {
          "id": "sy94rt",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "1x3w4f",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "imagemap_displayables"
        },
        {
          "id": "9xvqn6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "That's all for now."
        },
        {
          "id": "12aq71",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        },
        {
          "id": "fhw1tn",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "4tl47v",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screen_displayables_menu"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "fayxjj",
      "label": "screen_displayable_properties",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "h65wwa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are a few properties that every screen language displayable shares. Here, I'll demonstrate them for you."
        },
        {
          "id": "8w297q",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: First off, every screen language displayable supports the position properties. When the container a displayable is in supports it, you can use properties like align, anchor, pos, and so so on."
        },
        {
          "id": "l8cqwy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The at property applies a transform to the displayable, the same way the at clause in the show statement does."
        },
        {
          "id": "3evkri",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen at_example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "givec5",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "n6upvs",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example say_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "iq3gu2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The id property is mostly used with the say screen, which is used to show dialogue. Outside of the say screen, it isn't used much."
        },
        {
          "id": "tejy4q",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It tells Ren'Py which displayables are the background window, 'who' is speaking, and 'what' is being said. This used to apply per-Character styles, and help with auto-forward mode."
        },
        {
          "id": "dwuvbq",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "color: #c8ffc8"
        },
        {
          "id": "glt4lm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The style property lets you specify the style of a single displayable."
        },
        {
          "id": "t0in1f",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "style_prefix: green"
        },
        {
          "id": "2gs3r0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "style_prefix: yellow"
        },
        {
          "id": "nmfz8e",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "color: #ffffc8"
        },
        {
          "id": "927iqe",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The style_prefix property sets the prefix of the style that's used for a displayable and its children."
        },
        {
          "id": "6a9x1b",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, when the style_prefix property is 'green', the vbox has the 'green_vbox' style, and the text in it has the 'green_text' style."
        },
        {
          "id": "hcov59",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "6zotj3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are a few more properties than these, and you can find the rest in the documentation. But these are the ones you can expect to see in your game, in the default screens."
        },
        {
          "id": "pfahb2",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "fon0j7",
      "label": "add_displayable",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "ewhg8e",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Sometimes you'll have a displayable, like an image, that you want to add to a screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "a5emxc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: logo base"
        },
        {
          "id": "etaahe",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: This can be done using the add statement, which adds an image or other displayable to the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "z684ap",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are a few ways to refer to the image. If it's in the images directory or defined with the image statement, you can just put the name inside a quoted string."
        },
        {
          "id": "210f89",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: images/logo base.png"
        },
        {
          "id": "3vtqra",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: An image can also be referred to by its filename, relative to the game directory."
        },
        {
          "id": "9ctoee",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Other displayables can also be added using the add statement. Here, we add the Solid displayable, showing a solid block of color."
        },
        {
          "id": "rv98wx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: logo base"
        },
        {
          "id": "r3pdyr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In addition to the displayable, the add statement can be given transform properties. These can place or otherwise transform the displayable being added."
        },
        {
          "id": "4fb7xq",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: logo base"
        },
        {
          "id": "qtwprr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Of course, the add statement can also take the at property, letting you give it a more complex transform."
        },
        {
          "id": "hlm5ap",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "1q9lqz",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rvq3ar",
      "label": "text_displayable",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "zezgib",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The screen language text statement adds a text displayable to the screen. It takes one argument, the text to be displayed."
        },
        {
          "id": "43p646",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In addition to the common properties that all displayables take, text takes the text style properties. For example, size sets the size of the text."
        },
        {
          "id": "q128t8",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ answer = 42"
        },
        {
          "id": "cfclr3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The text displayable can also interpolate values enclosed in square brackets."
        },
        {
          "id": "tdxztd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When text is displayed in a screen using the text statement, variables defined in the screen take precedence over those defined outside it."
        },
        {
          "id": "ic5icy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Those variables may be parameters given to the screen, defined with the default or python statements, or set using the SetScreenVariable action."
        },
        {
          "id": "77ivge",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There's not much more to say about text in screens, as it works the same way as all other text in Ren'Py."
        },
        {
          "id": "pj0jcp",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "1dxjoh",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "115x6j",
      "label": "layout_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "iq6ccv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The layout displayables take other displayables and lay them out on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "gj3c9q",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "9rpjki",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "imczy8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "83i9bb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "n6uqwy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "b4fu7n",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: For example, the hbox displayable takes its children and lays them out horizontally."
        },
        {
          "id": "pjx530",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "g2ifli",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "d7dqr7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "e32mif",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "24h5mb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "i2rsic",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The vbox displayable is similar, except it takes its children and arranges them vertically."
        },
        {
          "id": "lr0y50",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Both of the boxes take the box style properties, the most useful of which is spacing, the amount of space to leave between children."
        },
        {
          "id": "w8n3qv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "4ny3f6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "ljie45",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "j86f53",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "j76wmn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "tykbef",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The grid displayable displays its children in a grid of equally-sized cells. It takes two arguments, the number of columns and the number of rows."
        },
        {
          "id": "swvns3",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The grid has to be full, or Ren'Py will produce an error. Notice how in this example, the empty cell is filled with a null."
        },
        {
          "id": "eqhzdt",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Like the boxes, grid uses the spacing property to specify the space between cells."
        },
        {
          "id": "jqhrfr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "f59elc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "lbytpr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "k2g8m1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "i21xbj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "bktqlz",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Grid also takes the transpose property, to make it fill top-to-bottom before it fills left-to-right."
        },
        {
          "id": "g345du",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "orzprg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "sbsdyw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "rfl22q",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "hqdyuw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "qzlom6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: And just to demonstrate that all cells are equally-sized, here's what happens when once child is bigger than the others."
        },
        {
          "id": "ir0jja",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "p5znau",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "iavkpr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "cfyh3i",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "s4ad35",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "tgq7dr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The fixed displayable displays the children using Ren'Py's normal placement algorithm. This lets you place displayables anywhere in the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "dqe5u5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By default, the layout expands to fill all the space available to it. To prevent that, we use the xsize and ysize properties to set its size in advance."
        },
        {
          "id": "mxipq2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "2uiteu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "762drr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "19usn6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "axa1ym",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "poeq4g",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When a non-layout displayable is given two or more children, it's not necessary to create a fixed. A fixed is automatically added, and the children are added to it."
        },
        {
          "id": "aey93p",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 1"
        },
        {
          "id": "lf84j0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 2"
        },
        {
          "id": "dlcm4r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 3"
        },
        {
          "id": "n1lkfk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 4"
        },
        {
          "id": "kuobxd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text: 5"
        },
        {
          "id": "xnjwk8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, there's one convenience to save space. When many displayables are nested, adding a layout to each could cause crazy indent levels."
        },
        {
          "id": "bbirgy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The has statement creates a layout, and then adds all further children of its parent to that layout. It's just a convenience to make screens more readable."
        },
        {
          "id": "p9j1qu",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "l298sz",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "093yak",
      "label": "window_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "7de8kw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In the default GUI that Ren'Py creates for a game, most user interface elements expect some sort of background."
        },
        {
          "id": "ryt9z7",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Without the background, text can be hard to read. While a frame isn't strictly required, many screens have one or more of them."
        },
        {
          "id": "xbkkrz",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: But when I add a background, it's much easier. That's why there are two displayables that are intended to give backgrounds to user interface elements."
        },
        {
          "id": "yoy2vu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The two displayables are frame and window. Frame is the one we use above, and it's designed to provide a background for arbitrary parts of the user interface."
        },
        {
          "id": "ns4juj",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example say_screen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "3unx5i",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: On the other hand, the window displayable is very specific. It's used to provide the text window. If you're reading what I'm saying, you're looking at the text window right now."
        },
        {
          "id": "mk7gfr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Both frames and windows can be given window style properties, allowing you to change things like the background, margins, and padding around the window."
        },
        {
          "id": "4engfb",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "hrg8i4",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "yqplo3",
      "label": "button_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "wpryod",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: One of the most flexible displayables is the button displayable, and its textbutton and imagebutton variants."
        },
        {
          "id": "aasn3w",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A button is a displayable that when selected runs an action. Buttons can be selected by clicking with the mouse, by touch, or with the keyboard and controller."
        },
        {
          "id": "x8uks0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Actions can do many things, like setting variables, showing screens, jumping to a label, or returning a value. There are many {a=https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/screen_actions.html}actions in the Ren'Py documentation{/a}, and you can also write your own."
        },
        {
          "id": "ijfdsg",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It's also possible to run actions when a button gains and loses focus."
        },
        {
          "id": "3l8rf8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A button takes another displayable as a child. Since that child can be a layout, it can take as many children as you want."
        },
        {
          "id": "bi90gu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In many cases, buttons will be given text. To make that easier, there's the textbutton displayable that takes the text as an argument."
        },
        {
          "id": "6hln95",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Since the textbutton displayable manages the style of the button text for you, it's the kind of button that's used most often in the default GUI."
        },
        {
          "id": "2ktjeo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "idle: logo bw"
        },
        {
          "id": "n4u9mx",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "hover: logo base"
        },
        {
          "id": "4x6moj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There's also the imagebutton, which takes displayables, one for each state the button can be in, and displays them as the button."
        },
        {
          "id": "zl7p33",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: An imagebutton gives you the most control over what a button looks like, but is harder to translate and won't look as good if the game window is resized."
        },
        {
          "id": "6xzlyh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "hover_sound: pong_beep.opus"
        },
        {
          "id": "2838wb",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text_idle_color: #c0c0c0"
        },
        {
          "id": "nbo4qk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "text_hover_color: #ffffff"
        },
        {
          "id": "mat1ov",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Buttons take Window style properties, that are used to specify the background, margins, and padding. They also take Button-specific properties, like a sound to play on hover."
        },
        {
          "id": "4rmmmy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When used with a button, style properties can be given prefixes like idle and hover to make the property change with the button state."
        },
        {
          "id": "yph0pe",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A text button also takes Text style properties, prefixed with text. These are applied to the text displayable it creates internally."
        },
        {
          "id": "78q8uc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "style: custom_button"
        },
        {
          "id": "nfcbtn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "style: custom_button"
        },
        {
          "id": "0zmi17",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "size_group: custom_button"
        },
        {
          "id": "0bsrkf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "hover_sound: pong_beep.opus"
        },
        {
          "id": "g6gdhv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "idle_color: #c0c0c0"
        },
        {
          "id": "p2lczv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "hover_color: #ffffff"
        },
        {
          "id": "ih1sgv",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Of course, it's pretty rare we'd ever customize a button in a screen like that. Instead, we'd create custom styles and tell Ren'Py to use them."
        },
        {
          "id": "8aj8an",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "n8c6ey",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        },
        {
          "id": "vs9m3n",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "7bpxvn",
      "label": "bar_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "y49zvs",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The bar and vbar displayables are flexible displayables that show bars representing a value. The value can be static, animated, or adjustable by the player."
        },
        {
          "id": "1l1r5g",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The value property gives a BarValue, which is an object that determines the bar's value and range. Here, a StaticValue sets the range to 100 and the value to 66, making a bar that's two thirds full."
        },
        {
          "id": "etfo9x",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A list of all the BarValues that can be used is found {a=https://www.renpy.org/doc/html/screen_actions.html#bar-values}in the Ren'Py documentation{/a}."
        },
        {
          "id": "qzk7ja",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: In this example, we give the frame the xsize property. If we didn't do that, the bar would expand to fill all available horizontal space."
        },
        {
          "id": "avtz5r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: There are a few different bar styles that are defined in the default GUI. The styles are selected by the style property, with the default selected by the value."
        },
        {
          "id": "igem5t",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The top style is the 'bar' style. It's used to display values that the player can't adjust, like a life or progress bar."
        },
        {
          "id": "qlra29",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The middle style is the 'slider' value. It's used for values the player is expected to adjust, like a volume preference."
        },
        {
          "id": "38ck3k",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, the bottom style is the 'scrollbar' style, which is used for horizontal scrollbars. When used as a scrollbar, the thumb in the center changes size to reflect the visible area of a viewport."
        },
        {
          "id": "covmmr",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The vbar displayable is similar to the bar displayable, except it uses vertical styles - 'vbar', 'vslider', and 'vscrollbar' - by default."
        },
        {
          "id": "qkewwn",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Bars take the Bar style properties, which can customize the look and feel greatly. Just look at the difference between the bar, slider, and scrollbar styles."
        },
        {
          "id": "wop2fu",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "imo2ck",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1x3w4f",
      "label": "imagemap_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "6pmbb1",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Imagemaps use two or more images to show buttons and bars. Let me start by showing you an example of an imagemap in action."
        },
        {
          "id": "o3tiep",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "idle: imagemap ground"
        },
        {
          "id": "x8fkz0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "hover: imagemap hover"
        },
        {
          "id": "uiohwz",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "ujh847",
          "transition": "none",
          "_targetLabel": "imagemap_example"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ujh847",
      "label": "imagemap_example",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "hdbi50",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "screen"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "t6w7v4",
      "label": "swimming",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "c54vko",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You chose swimming."
        },
        {
          "id": "tssyty",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Swimming seems like a lot of fun, but I didn't bring my bathing suit with me."
        },
        {
          "id": "smh05i",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "0abc6y",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "imagemap_done"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "reuv6e",
      "label": "science",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "y0ybcj",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You chose science."
        },
        {
          "id": "qpraxy",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: I've heard that some schools have a competitive science team, but to me research is something that can't be rushed."
        },
        {
          "id": "phzqyy",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "0abc6y",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "imagemap_done"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "cvpyvb",
      "label": "art",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "jnp4om",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You chose art."
        },
        {
          "id": "qptsuf",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Really good background art is hard to make, which is why so many games use filtered photographs. Maybe you can change that."
        },
        {
          "id": "2iw4s9",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "0abc6y",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "imagemap_done"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1v6mg7",
      "label": "home",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "fl4yd4",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: You chose to go home."
        },
        {
          "id": "0hux87",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": "0abc6y",
          "transition": "dissolve",
          "_targetLabel": "imagemap_done"
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "0abc6y",
      "label": "imagemap_done",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "w7i53l",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Anyway..."
        },
        {
          "id": "v3rssh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To demonstrate how imagemaps are put together, I'll show you the five images that make up a smaller imagemap."
        },
        {
          "id": "hp3g5i",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show imagemap volume idle:"
        },
        {
          "id": "wh72e0",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "juvye5",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The idle image is used for the background of the imagemap, for hotspot buttons that aren't focused or selected, and for the empty part of an unfocused bar."
        },
        {
          "id": "07g57j",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show imagemap volume hover:"
        },
        {
          "id": "si9jun",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "2noc4b",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The hover image is used for hotspots that are focused but not selected, and for the empty part of a focused bar."
        },
        {
          "id": "elcdxo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Notice how both the bar and button are highlighted in this image. When we display them as part of a screen, only one of them will show up as focused."
        },
        {
          "id": "k6hkzd",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show imagemap volume selected_idle:"
        },
        {
          "id": "dyr0fq",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "hfplg6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Selected images like this selected_idle image are used for parts of the bar that are filled, and for selected buttons, like the current screen and a checked checkbox."
        },
        {
          "id": "myvtd4",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show imagemap volume selected_hover:"
        },
        {
          "id": "8fp4u9",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "rpnmgm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's the selected_hover image. The button here will never be shown, since it will never be marked as selected."
        },
        {
          "id": "4n86b4",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "show imagemap volume insensitive:"
        },
        {
          "id": "ueezfx",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "0t6kam",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, an insensitive image can be given, which is used when a hotspot can't be interacted with."
        },
        {
          "id": "b65y8d",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide imagemap",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "ivl7ml",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "txs93o",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Imagemaps aren't limited to just images. Any displayable can be used where an image is expected."
        },
        {
          "id": "elzmvc",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "idle: imagemap volume idle"
        },
        {
          "id": "hmhpfm",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "hover: imagemap volume hover"
        },
        {
          "id": "zrhjc6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "selected_idle: imagemap volume selected_idle"
        },
        {
          "id": "6nzets",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "selected_hover: imagemap volume selected_hover"
        },
        {
          "id": "heg05f",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "insensitive: imagemap volume insensitive"
        },
        {
          "id": "wcej5r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's an imagemap built using those five images. Now that it's an imagemap, you can interact with it if you want to."
        },
        {
          "id": "9q8vyk",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "auto: imagemap volume %s"
        },
        {
          "id": "i7uguh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To make this a little more concise, we can replace the five images with the auto property, which replaces '%%s' with 'idle', 'hover', 'selected_idle', 'selected_hover', or 'insensitive' as appropriate."
        },
        {
          "id": "trd1ip",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Feel free to omit the selected and insensitive images if your game doesn't need them. Ren'Py will use the idle or hover images to replace them."
        },
        {
          "id": "14ml42",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The hotspot and hotbar statements describe areas of the imagemap that should act as buttons or bars, respectively."
        },
        {
          "id": "7ti1h4",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Both take the coordinates of the area, in (x, y, width, height) format."
        },
        {
          "id": "l343r2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A hotspot takes an action that is run when the hotspot is activated. It can also take actions that are run when it's hovered and unhovered, just like a button can."
        },
        {
          "id": "95xluw",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A hotbar takes a BarValue object that describes how full the bar is, and the range of values the bar should display, just like a bar and vbar does."
        },
        {
          "id": "z4x83y",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen volume_imagemap_auto_example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "2w7rgg",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example imagemap",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "p7nlgw",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "v5mz6k",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A useful pattern is to define a screen with an imagemap that has hotspots that jump to labels, and call that using the call screen statement."
        },
        {
          "id": "bp4ke6",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: That's what we did in the school example I showed before. Here's the script for it. It's long, but the imagemap itself is fairly simple."
        },
        {
          "id": "nnhrk6",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "3445pa",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Imagemaps have pluses and minuses. On one hand, they are easy for a designer to create, and can look very good. At the same time, they can be hard to translate, and text baked into images may be blurry when the window is scaled."
        },
        {
          "id": "bczt7r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It's up to you and your team to decide if imagemaps are right for your project."
        },
        {
          "id": "4w3r7l",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "nkaa56",
      "label": "viewport_displayables",
      "bg": null,
      "music": null,
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "ct4bl8",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Sometimes, you'll want to display something bigger than the screen. That's what the viewport displayable is for."
        },
        {
          "id": "bp5suh",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: bg band"
        },
        {
          "id": "6czjoa",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "6iilib",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Here's an example of a simple viewport, used to display a single image that's far bigger than the screen. Since the viewport will expand to the size of the screen, we use the xysize property to make it smaller."
        },
        {
          "id": "2gtlan",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: By default the viewport can't be moved, so we give the draggable, mousewheel, and arrowkeys properties to allow it to be moved in multiple ways."
        },
        {
          "id": "tqvs4k",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: bg band"
        },
        {
          "id": "tr2fx9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When I give the viewport the edgescroll property, the viewport automatically scrolls when the mouse is near its edges. The two numbers are the size of the edges, and the speed in pixels per second."
        },
        {
          "id": "kp8tre",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "scrollbars: both"
        },
        {
          "id": "u503zo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: bg band"
        },
        {
          "id": "j33ceq",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "6ys19r",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Giving the viewport the scrollbars property surrounds it with scrollbars. The scrollbars property can take 'both', 'horizontal', and 'vertical' as values."
        },
        {
          "id": "wmeril",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The spacing property controls the space between the viewport and its scrollbars, in pixels."
        },
        {
          "id": "jdx4ca",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "scrollbars: both"
        },
        {
          "id": "2cet4h",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: bg band"
        },
        {
          "id": "zj5g3u",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "tlyhir",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The xinitial and yinitial properties set the initial amount of scrolling, as a fraction of the amount that can be scrolled."
        },
        {
          "id": "yo97ze",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "scrollbars: both"
        },
        {
          "id": "vlctd9",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "add: logo base"
        },
        {
          "id": "vvn9ci",
          "type": "effect",
          "kind": "dissolve",
          "dur": 0.5
        },
        {
          "id": "viapls",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Finally, there's the vpgrid displayable. It combines a viewport and a grid into a single displayable, except it's more efficient than either, since it doesn't have to draw every child."
        },
        {
          "id": "yzab36",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: It takes the cols and rows properties, which give the number of rows and columns of children. If one is omitted, Ren'Py figures it out from the other and the number of children."
        },
        {
          "id": "oimf9x",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "scu1zq",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rumr8u",
      "label": "tutorial_video",
      "bg": null,
      "music": "sunflower-slow-drag.ogg",
      "events": [
        {
          "id": "78za4f",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Ren'Py supports playing movies. There are two ways of doing this."
        },
        {
          "id": "rodda2",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The first way allows you to show a movie as an image, along with every other image that's displayed on the screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "wiynnu",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example movie_image",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "3q9p24",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: To do this, we first have to define an image to be a Movie displayable. Movie displayables take a movie to play, and can be given position properties."
        },
        {
          "id": "nmo3ta",
          "type": "music",
          "music": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "duy4li",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example movie_play",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "4biwsx",
          "type": "wait",
          "dur": 0.25
        },
        {
          "id": "0dq1ek",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "launch behind eileen",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "20r2bu",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: Then, we can show the movie displayable, which starts the movie playing."
        },
        {
          "id": "ruvkdx",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide launch",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "jopsz0",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: When we no longer want to play the movie, we can hide it."
        },
        {
          "id": "kqpwna",
          "type": "image",
          "image": "example movie_cutscene",
          "side": "center"
        },
        {
          "id": "kuhpbo",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: The other way to show a movie is with the renpy.movie_cutscene python function. This shows the movie fullscreen, either until it ends or until the user clicks."
        },
        {
          "id": "xnkf5x",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "hide screen example",
          "_isSkipped": true
        },
        {
          "id": "2b33hf",
          "type": "raw",
          "raw_code": "$ renpy.movie_cutscene(\"oa4_launch.webm\")"
        },
        {
          "id": "d4a3wd",
          "type": "narration",
          "text": "e: A Movie displayable can also take a mask with an alpha channel, which lets you make movie sprites. But that's more complicated, so I'll stop here for now."
        },
        {
          "id": "wme0rc",
          "type": "music",
          "music": "sunflower-slow-drag.ogg"
        },
        {
          "id": "cthfiy",
          "type": "jump",
          "scene_id": null,
          "_isReturn": true
        }
      ],
      "scene_ids": []
    }
  ],
